": "A late tightening in independent polling or a shift among LDS and independent voters toward Biggs could narrow the race considerably.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Katie Hobbs (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's governor race pits incumbent Democrat Katie Hobbs against Republican Andy Biggs, a conservative former congressman. Polling since midsummer has consistently shown Hobbs ahead, with several surveys giving her double-digit leads among likely voters, though a few Republican-leaning polls put the race much closer. She has also raised roughly three times as much money as Biggs and holds a cash advantage heading into the fall.\n\nRaters across the spectrum call the race Lean D, with one rating it Likely D, and prediction markets give Hobbs roughly a 90% chance of winning. Arizona's slight Republican tilt and the potential for a closer race as voters focus on the two candidates keep this from being a safe Democratic seat, but the weight of polling, money, and market pricing points to Hobbs as the clear favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9144, "margin": 7.07}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9047, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9201, "margin": 7.5}]}